One of my recent projects is available on GitHub, where I documented the development and
implementation of an EEG-Based Sleep Stage Classification system using Deep Learning.

The primary motivation behind this project was to explore the application of artificial intelligence in healthcare, specifically in analyzing EEG signals to automate sleep stage classification.
Sleep analysis traditionally requires significant manual effort from medical professionals,
and I wanted to investigate how deep learning techniques could improve efficiency and accuracy in this process.

The most challenging concept to explain clearly was the transformation of raw EEG signals into meaningful inputs for a deep learning model.
EEG data is highly complex, noisy, and difficult for non-specialists to interpret. I focused on presenting the preprocessing pipeline, feature extraction process, 
and neural network architecture in a way that was both technically accurate and easy to understand for readers with different levels of expertise.

Through this project, I gained valuable experience in machine learning, signal processing,
data preprocessing, and model evaluation, while also improving my ability to communicate complex technical concepts through clear documentation.
